I followed the example of Dragon Scales for this:  the cloaks drop
from a rare mythical creature.  Dragon scales are also a game-logic
precedent for items giving MC0 when you would logically expect more.
Unlike dragon scales, enchanting a Leo Nemaeus hide doesn't do
anything special, and if you polymorph you tear out of it like a
normal cloak instead of turning into a son of Typhon.  I may
eventually change that.

> * Greaves are armor that you wear on the shins (so,
>   in NetHack terms, it would use the top half of the
>   boot slot -- you could wear it together with low boots
>   or iron shoes but not with most other boots).  Wearing
>   only greaves or only low boots leaves you vulnerable
>   to xan, but if you have both it's equivalent to high
>   boots.  The advantage is that you can enchant both,
>   so you can get an extra 4-5 AC bonus, but the cost
>   is that you don't get the various bonus features the
>   more popular models of boot offer (water walking
>   or speed, mainly).  There could be elven greaves,
>   for an extra couple of points of enchantability.

I suspect this trade off between lowering your AC and useful
extrinsics could be more easily achieved by simply making greaves a
new type of boot, although there is greater mix-and-match potential
your way.  Greaves as a new boot would also require fewer spare
enchant armor scrolls for the player to take advantage of.

